Unreadable colors in rteval --help output
==========================================

Symptom
-------
Running `rteval --help` (or seeing a Python traceback) shows option names,
metavars, or headings in very light, washed-out colors that are hard or
impossible to read. This typically happens in terminals with a *light*
(white) background.

Cause
-----
This is NOT an rteval bug. Starting with Python 3.14, the standard library
colorizes argparse `--help` output and tracebacks by default. argparse styles
option strings and metavars in *bold* colors. Many terminals map bold text to
their "bright" color palette, and the standard bright colors (bright green,
yellow, cyan, white) are designed for dark backgrounds -- on a white
background they are nearly invisible.

Fixes
-----
Pick whichever suits you.

1. Disable colored output entirely (works in any terminal, any program):

     export NO_COLOR=1

   or, to affect only Python:

     export PYTHON_COLORS=0

   Add the line to ~/.bashrc to make it permanent. This turns the colors off
   rather than fixing them.

2. Keep colors, but stop your terminal from washing out bold text. The option
   name varies by terminal emulator; look for a setting like
   "Show bold text in bright colors" / "Bold is bright" and turn it OFF, or
   enable "Use colors from system theme".

   - GNOME Terminal / Tilix: Preferences -> (profile) -> Colors ->
     uncheck "Show bold text in bright colors".
   - Konsole: Settings -> Edit Current Profile -> Appearance; use a color
     scheme intended for a light background.
   - xfce4-terminal: the relevant setting lives in xfconf, not just the
     Preferences dialog. From a terminal run:

       xfconf-query -c xfce4-terminal -p /color-bold-is-bright -s false

     (If that does not fully help, also try:
       xfconf-query -c xfce4-terminal -p /color-use-theme -s true )

3. Use a terminal color scheme / palette designed for a light background, so
   the bright colors remain dark enough to read.

If none of the above helps, please report it to the rteval mailing list
(linux-rt-users@vger.kernel.org) with your terminal emulator name and version.
